    "rawContent": [
      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Now for §四 配对设备指纹. Design considerations:\n\n\"配对设备指纹（人眼可核）+ 键变更 fail-closed 标记\"\n\nLikely intent: \n1. Every paired device gets a human-verifiable fingerprint (e.g. derived from deviceId + tokenHash), displayed on both computer (pair devices list) and phone (after redeem), so the user can compare visually to confirm \"this is my phone\".\n2. Key-change fail-closed: if a device's token (key) changes — e.g. the stored tokenHash doesn't match presented token — the device gets marked (compromised) rather than silently re-binding.\n\nHmm, \"键变更 fail-closed 标记\": When a paired device presents a token, `verifyDeviceToken` searches all devices for matching hash. If no match, returns false (403). That's already fail-closed. But the \"marker\" part: we need to record that a known device present a *different* key? But how do we identify \"known device\" without any stable identifier? Maybe the phone sends `deviceId` along with the token; if deviceId matches but tokenHash doesn't → key changed → mark device `keyChangedAt` and refuse (fail-closed) until re-pair/revoke.\n\nActually simpler interpretation: when a device is re-paired (same deviceId? no, new random id)... Hmm.\n\nLet me look for related design intent. Maybe there's a 批 24/25 doc or a \"剩余事项\" doc mentioning 指纹. Search docs for \"人眼\" / \"可核\" / \"fail-closed\"."
      }
