        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Now, the middleware `createAuthMiddleware` is in auth.ts. Let me read it.\n\nFor §四, a pragmatic design that fits \"server side only\" and is testable:\n\nFingerprint: derive from `deviceId` + `tokenHash` a short human-readable string, e.g. base32-ish 3 groups of 4 (12 chars) or `XXXX-XXXX-XXXX`. Format similar to SSH fingerprint but human-readable. Since tokenHash is secret-derived, exposing a truncated fingerprint of sha256(deviceId + tokenHash) is fine (it doesn't leak token; only 48 bits shown).\n\nWhere surfaced:\n- `GET /api/v1/pair/devices` → each device gets `fingerprint`.\n- `POST /api/v1/pair/redeem` → returns `fingerprint` + `deviceId` so phone can show it (cross-repo contract; but adding field is additive).\n\nKey-change fail-closed marker:\nOption A (store integrity): on `load()`, verify each device's stored `fingerprint` against recomputed value; if mismatched (someone edited the store file → key changed), set `integrityIssue: true`, and `verifyDeviceToken` refuses those devices (fail-closed). Expose `keyChanged` in list.\n\nHmm, but \"键变更\" more naturally refers to the device's key (token) being changed. In this codebase keys only change when a new device is registered. Nothing rotates keys.\n\nLet me think about what a \"key change\" could mean operationally: Same device (same deviceId) pairs again → new token issued → old tokenHash replaced. But redeem always creates a new random deviceId, so no.\n\nGiven no mobile side in this repo, the most defensible server-side implementation of \"键变更 fail-closed 标记\":\n- Store per-device `fingerprint` (derived from deviceId + tokenHash) at registration.\n- On load/verify, recompute; mismatched fingerprint = the stored key material was changed outside the app (file tampered / restored from another machine's backup) → mark device `keyChangedAt` and refuse verification (fail-closed: 403) until user revokes/re-pairs; list API exposes `keyChanged: true` and `fingerprint`.\n\nThat's a coherent, testable, minimal implementation: \"键变更 → 标记 + fail-closed\".\n\nAdditionally, verifyDeviceToken can detect \"same deviceId presented with a different token\" if the client sends deviceId. But the current contract only sends the bearer token. Adding a required header would break the other repo. So skip.\n\nI'll implement:\n1. `deviceFingerprint(deviceId, tokenHash)` — pure function: sha256(\"lifediary-device-v1|deviceId|tokenHash\") → take 12 hex chars grouped as 4-4-4 uppercase (human-readable, ~48 bits). Actually \"人眼可核\" means easy to compare: 8-12 chars in groups.\n2. StoredDevice gains `fingerprint?: string` (legacy records have none → compute on load and persist? For legacy devices, compute lazily and save, so old files migrate).\n3. On load: for each device, if fingerprint exists and mismatch recomputed → set in-memory `keyChanged: true` (not persisted as tamper evidence?). Better: persist `keyChangedAt` so marker survives restart. But writing during load... load() is called in constructor; could mark in memory and persist on next save. Simpler: compute `keyChangedAt = now` and save once if any mismatch.\n\nHmm, careful: if someone tampers with tokenHash, then save() would... we must not overwrite the tampered hash with the original (we don't know it). We just mark. Then verifyDeviceToken refuses. Revoke works. Re-pair creates a new device.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: legacy devices without fingerprint get fingerprint computed and stored — that's a migration, fine.\n\n4. `verifyDeviceToken`: skip devices with `keyChangedAt` (fail-closed).\n5. `listDevices()`: include `fingerprint` and `keyChanged` (bool) — index.ts maps fields explicitly, so update there too (both `GET /api/v1/pair/devices` versions in index.ts).\n6. `redeem` returns `fingerprint` and `deviceId` (deviceId currently returned in RedeemResult but index.ts redeem response doesn't include it! It returns deviceToken/name/version). Adding `deviceId` and `fingerprint` is additive.\n7. Rollback switch: `PAIR_DEVICE_FINGERPRINT=false` → no fingerprint in responses and no fail-closed marker (behave as today). Per AGENTS rule \"新能力给回滚开关\".\n\nUnit tests in a new `pairing.test.ts`:\n- fingerprint deterministic, 人眼可核 format, differs per device.\n- listDevices never leaks tokenHash.\n- tamper tokenHash in file → new store loads → device marked keyChanged, verifyDeviceToken(old token) false (fail-closed), fingerprint still present.\n- legacy record without fingerprint migrates.\n- switch off → no fingerprint key / no fail-closed.\n\nWait: \"键变更 fail-closed 标记\" — does tampering with the store file count as \"键变更\"? In practical terms, the token hash IS the key. If tokenHash changes without a matching fingerprint, that means the key was changed out-of-band. Yes.\n\nHmm, but what about legitimate key rotation? There's no rotation API; re-pair creates a new device. So key change = anomaly. Fine.\n\nNow §一 phone worker reverify. Spec (§三 of batch 25):\n- 3.2 Before dispatch() actually sends out, check again: whether task has been canceled / timed out / claimed by another worker; if any → do not send request, settle with corresponding terminal state (canceled call never produces side effects).\n- 3.3 Similarly, before writing result back, check again: call already canceled → discard result, don't write back (log one line, only id not content).\n- 3.4 Switch `PHONE_ENSURE_ACTIVE` (default true; false → today's behavior).\n- 3.5 Unit tests: canceled → not sent / timed out → not sent / already claimed → not re-sent / canceled → result discarded / switch bypass.\n\nWait — \"dispatch() 真该发出前\" — in phone-worker.ts, dispatch is the transport used by chancellor to send a task to the phone. It queues the task and waits for poll. \"真该发出\" = when a poll takes a task (flushPoll) — that's the moment the request actually goes out to the phone. So the recheck should happen in flushPoll/nextQueuedTask: before handing a task to a poll, verify the task is still active (signal not aborted, not timed out, not canceled).\n\nBut how do we know \"task canceled\"? The signal from chancellor — if aborted, `finish` already removed pending and rejected. So after abort, pending.delete happened → nextQueuedTask won't find it. Timer expiry also deletes. \"已被别的 worker 认领\" — flushPoll removes poll and the task is removed from ready; but task stays in pending until result. If another poll comes... `nextQueuedTask` only returns tasks still in `ready`. Once handed to a poll, the task is removed from ready (shift). So it can't be claimed twice. Hmm.\n\nWhat's actually missing? The critical case: `dispatch()` checks `signal?.aborted` at entry and registers abort listener. But between \"task queued in ready\" and \"poll arrives\", the abort may fire → onAbort → finish → pending.delete + ready.splice. So ready is cleaned. So the race is already handled?\n\nThe race window: flushPoll resolves the poll with the task at the same moment abort fires. In JS single-threaded, no interleaving inside sync code. But after `poll.resolve(task)` the HTTP response is produced asynchronously: `received` set, promise resolves, then handler continues to `c.json(toWireTask(received))`. If abort happens after `poll.resolve` but before response written, `finish` → reject → pending deleted — but the task is already sent in the HTTP response. Then the phone executes a canceled task. That's the real gap: \"执行前重验\" must be on the *phone side* — but phone is a different repo. Server side can't do more than... unless we add a \"preflight/confirm\" endpoint the phone calls before executing. That would be §一: \"手机 worker 执行前重验\" — i.e. add `POST /api/v1/worker/claim` or a `GET /worker/task/:id` check that the phone calls right before executing, and the server tells it \"still active\" or \"canceled/timeout\".\n\nHmm. But the user's key facts say \"phone-worker.ts 有 UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS 与 abort signal；dispatch() 在 42 行附近\" and the batch 25 spec says \"在 dispatch() 真该发出前，再查一次\". That suggests a server-side check inside dispatch.\n\nHmm, wait. Let's reconsider: maybe there's another direction — the *harness* dispatches to phone worker, and the phone polls. \"执行前重验调用还活着\" — maybe the phone-side code is in this repo? Let me check: is there a phone worker client implementation in this repo? \"手机 worker\" is the Android app. But maybe there's a probe script `phone-worker-probe.ts` simulating a phone.\n\nLet me read `scripts/phone-worker-probe.ts` and `worker-shell.ts`, and check chancellor's PhoneTransport / dispatch semantics (maybe dispatch is invoked by a worker loop in this repo, e.g. relay or partner-work on the phone?).\n\nActually `registerPhoneTransport` is imported from chancellor. In chancellor, the phone transport is used when the local machine decides to delegate a task to a paired phone (e.g., host-priority \"满溢外流\"). The dispatch() call sends the task to the phone. So \"在 dispatch() 真该发出前再查一次\" means: before enqueuing/sending, re-check whether the task should still be run (canceled/timed out/claimed). But the \"task\" here is created by chancellor...\n\nAlternatively — maybe \"手机 worker\" refers to `worker-shell.ts` (受限 shell)? No, that's adb.\n\nLet me look at chancellor's phone transport interface and how dispatch gets called, plus the probe script."
      }
    ],
