      "reasoning": "So on the server side, dispatch(task, signal) is called from the phone employee. The abort signal comes from chancellor when the ticket is stopped.\n\n\"执行前重验\" — but the phone is a separate repo. What can the server do? The server knows taskId → pending entry with its task and the abort listener. The \"actual send\" is the poll response. So the reverify must happen at poll time: before returning a task to the phone, check it's still needed. Since finish() removes the pending entry on abort/timeout, `nextQueuedTask` already skips missing entries. And abort events remove from ready via `finish`.\n\nSo where is the hole? Consider: dispatch() with an already-aborted signal → rejects immediately, no pending entry. Good.\n\nHmm, but the user says \"phone-worker.ts 有 UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS 与 abort signal；dispatch() 在 42 行附近\" and batch 25 §3.2 \"在 dispatch() 真该发出前，再查一次\". Maybe the intent is simpler than I think: add a \"still active?\" gate function that dispatch consults both at enqueue and at hand-off. Even if today's code is mostly correct, we add explicit checks + tests to pin behavior:\n\n1. At `dispatch()` entry: if signal aborted → reject (exists).\n2. At hand-off (`flushPoll` → task given to poll): re-check `isTaskActive(taskId)` — i.e., pending has entry AND entry.task === task AND not timed out (Date.now() < deadline) AND signal not aborted. If not active → don't send, and settle appropriately (abort → reject; expiry → timeout error; canceled → remove).\n3. At result write-back (`/worker/result`): re-check pending still exists and is active; if the task was canceled/aborted in the meantime → discard result, return 409/200? Spec: \"调用已取消 → 丢弃结果、不写回（记一条日志，只记 id 不记内容）\". Since pending.delete on abort, `entry` would be missing → 404 already. Hmm.\n\nWait — \"写回\" here means writing result.md via chancellor. That happens in chancellor `outcomeFromResult` after dispatch resolves. Once dispatch rejects on abort, no write happens. So already fine... \n\nUnless the cancellation happens *after* the phone posts the result but *before* the promise resolves/chancellor writes. JS is single threaded; resolve → microtask. Abort listener would have removed pending. Actually the race: phone posts result → entry.resolve(result) → finish() → resolve promise. Then chancellor writes result.md. If abort happens right after resolve but before the write... the promise was already resolved with result; the write occurs. Hmm, abort between? Microtasks run before next macrotask (HTTP request), so abort can't interleave. Meh.\n\nI think the task is more about *the code path where a canceled task is still queued*: \n- Task A queued in `ready`. Abort fires → `finish` removes from ready. OK.\n- But what about `signal?.addEventListener` when signal already aborted after dispatch entry but before the promise executor registers? Not possible synchronously.\n\nHonestly the existing implementation seems already correct for most cases. But the spec explicitly asks for \"执行前重验\" as a belt-and-braces guard, with a switch and tests. There IS one real hole: **`flushPoll` picks a poll from any device and hands it whatever task is first**, with no per-device task affinity, and no validity re-check at hand-off. Another hole: after hand-off, the task is no longer in `ready`, but it remains in `pending`; if the phone never responds and the poll response write fails, no retry. Not our concern.\n\nAnother real hole: `dispatch` timeout uses `task.budgetMs` — if a task sits queued for a long time, timeout fires and `finish` rejects; fine.\n\nReal hole #2: When abort fires after `poll.resolve(task)` but before the response is actually flushed to the network (await returns later), the phone still receives the task and executes it. Server can't un-send. The only way to reduce side effects is to make the phone check before executing — cross-repo. Not this batch.\n\nSo server-side, the plausible deliverable:\n- A `claim gate` at hand-off: `flushPoll`/`nextQueuedTask` verify each candidate is still active before resolving the poll; drop stale entries from ready as it scans (already done via pending lookup).\n- A result gate: `/worker/result` verifies the task is still active before resolving; if canceled/timeout → discard (log id only) and return 409 `task_inactive` (or 200 with discarded:true?). Choose 409? Hmm, \"丢弃结果、不写回\" — response to phone can be 409 {ok:false,error:\"task_inactive\"}. But contract change; phone might treat unknown codes as failure. Since phone does `fetch(.../result)` and probably ignores body. Keep 200 {ok:true, discarded:true}? Safer: 200 with `{ok:true, applied:false}`. Hmm.\n\nWait. Maybe I'm overанализируя. Let me look at the http-probe to see whether phone worker endpoints are covered, and look at existing tests for phone-worker (worker-shell.test.ts is different). There's no phone-worker.test.ts. I'll create one.\n\nDesign decision for §一 (server-side, minimal, testable):\n\nAdd to phone-worker.ts:\n- `activeTasks` bookkeeping: each PendingEntry gains `{ task, resolve, deadlineAt, signal? }`.\n- Export a small pure-ish helper `taskInactiveReason(entry, now)` returning \"canceled\" | \"expired\" | null? Aborted state is expressed by the signal's aborted flag (need to keep signal in entry).\n- At hand-off (`flushPoll`): before resolving poll with a task, re-check `pending.get(taskId)` exists and `!entry.signal?.aborted` and `now < deadlineAt`; if invalid, `finish`-remove and continue to next.\n- At result: `/worker/result` re-check: entry exists, not aborted, not expired → resolve; else discard + log `[phone] 丢弃迟到结果 task=<id> reason=...` (id only, no content), return `{ ok: true, applied: false }` (200 so phone doesn't retry). Hmm, \"不写回\" is what matters.\n- Switch `PHONE_ENSURE_ACTIVE` (default true) → when false, skip the re-checks entirely (today's behavior).\n- Unit tests: need to test through HTTP (Hono app) + registering transport. Since module-level `pending/ready/polls` are module singletons, tests can mount a fresh Hono app and call the transport via `registerPhoneTransport`... importing phone-worker registers globally. To dispatch, we can import `registerPhoneTransport` and call the transport? The module registers its own transport at import; we can grab it by re-registering? Better: export a test hook `__dispatchForTest`? Hmm, that's ugly.\n\nAlternative: export `phoneTransport()` from chancellor (`phoneTransport()` requires registered). In test, import phone-worker (registers), then `import { phoneTransport } from chancellor` and call `phoneTransport().dispatch(task, signal)`. But module state persists across tests in the same file; need reset. Could add an exported `__resetPhoneWorkerForTest()` guarded? There is likely a pattern in repo for such resets. Let me check other tests for module state resets.\n\nSimpler: test cancel-before-send: \n1. dispatch(task, signal) → queued, no poll.\n2. abort → promise rejects; then poll → 204 (task never delivered).\n3. Assert phone never received: poll returns 204.\n\nTest cancel-before-poll when task was already handed: not relevant.\n\nTest timeout: dispatch with budgetMs=20 → after 30ms, poll → 204.\n\nTest \"already claimed not re-sent\": poll once gets task; second poll → 204 (needs waiting 1s timeout... poll with timeoutS=1 → 1s. acceptable).\n\nTest result discard: dispatch → poll → get task → abort → POST result → applied:false / 404.\n\nTest switch off: with PHONE_ENSURE_ACTIVE=false, a result for an expired task... hmm, behavior today: `pending` entry removed on timeout so 404 anyway. The switch's observable difference matters only where the new check differs from old. If my new checks are redundant with existing finish() cleanup, the switch has no observable effect and tests can't pin it → bad design.\n\nSo I need the new check to be *additional* to existing cleanup. Where is it actually non-redundant?\n\nKey insight: abort listener is registered only if `signal` is defined. In `dispatch`, `signal?.addEventListener`. Always passed by phone employee (signal param). But if someone calls dispatch without signal, no cleanup beyond timeout. Fine.\n\nNon-redundant case: **task handed to poll → phone executes → abort → phone posts result**. On abort, `finish` deletes pending and rejects. Phone posts result → 404 unknown_task. So discarded. Already fail-safe, but the *log* is missing. Also \"已被别的 worker 认领\" — if the same task were delivered to two polls (can't happen).\n\nNon-redundant case #2: **timeout fires between poll hand-off and the response flush**? Same as abort.\n\nNon-redundant: **abort arrives while a poll is already waiting and the task is being handed off in the same tick**: `flushPoll` resolves the poll synchronously; abort later → reject while the HTTP response has the task. Phone executes. Not fixable server-side except the phone re-checking.\n\nGiven that, maybe §一 is actually about adding a **new preflight endpoint for the phone**: `POST /api/v1/worker/claim` (or `GET /worker/tasks/:id/alive`) that the phone calls right before executing, server answers `{active:true|false, reason}`. Then \"取消后不产生副作用\" is enforceable: after cancel, the preflight says inactive → phone won't execute. But the phone repo changes are out of scope... yet the server-side endpoint + tests can be done now, and phone adopts later. That's the only server-side thing that actually changes behavior. And batch 25 §3.2 says \"在 dispatch() 真该发出前，再查一次\" — hmm, not matching.\n\nHold on. Maybe I'm misreading: maybe \"dispatch()\" refers to something in the *phone client* code inside this repo — e.g., `worker-shell.ts`? No. Let me search the repo for another `dispatch` in relation to phone. Also maybe `relay` client dispatches tasks to phone.\n\nLet me grep \"worker/poll\" across repo (including Android? no).\n\nActually — maybe there IS a phone-side implementation in this repo: `desktop/` is Go shell; maybe `ts/apps/server/scripts/phone-sim`? Let me check `scripts/` and whether there's a phone worker client TS used by the desktop shell.\n\nI should ask the user. The task book is missing, and the exact intent of §一 and §四 is ambiguous. I've spent a fair amount of investigation; asking one focused question now is warranted.\n\nBut before asking, let me quickly check git log on batch26-all to see if there are prior commits hinting at planned content (branch name batch26-all, HEAD 51de48b).",
