      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "`phoneTransport` isn't re-exported from employee/index or chancellor index. I could import directly from the phone.ts path in the test:\n`import { phoneTransport } from \"../../../../../chancellor/src/employee/phone.ts\";`\nThat works and doesn't change chancellor. But AGENTS says don't touch chancellor (read-only fine).\n\nAlternatively export a test hook from phone-worker: `export const phoneWorkerTransport = transport;`? Simpler and self-contained; also useful for the probe? I'll export `export const phoneTransportForTest`? Naming... Just import from chancellor employee/phone.ts in test — no production surface change. Good.\n\nBut wait: does the test's import of phone-worker register a transport, and then `phoneTransport()` returns it? Yes, registered at module import.\n\nTest structure. Note: node:test runs tests in a file sequentially by default? In Node's test runner, top-level tests run sequentially unless `concurrency` is set. Yes, default concurrency is 1 for subtests in the same file? Actually `node --test` runs files in parallel (one process each), but tests within a file run sequentially by default. Good.\n\nBut the package's test script is `tsx --test tsx --test src/...` — weird duplication but works.\n\nTimer concerns: dispatch with budgetMs 1500 for delivery tests to avoid accidental timeout. For timeout test use 20ms.\n\nPoll request: `app.request(\"/api/v1/worker/poll\", { method: \"POST\", headers: {\"content-type\":\"application/json\"}, body: JSON.stringify({timeoutS:1}) })`. requires `c.req.json()` works.\n\nAuth: mountPhoneWorker doesn't check auth; fine.\n\nFor the \"second poll gets 204\" test, timeoutS 1 → 1s wait; acceptable but adds 1s. Use timeoutS: 1 (min 1). OK.\n\nTest 4 result discard: abort after delivery; POST result → expect 200 and `applied===false`. Then check that the dispatch promise rejected with AbortError.\n\nTest 5 switch off: set process.env.PHONE_ENSURE_ACTIVE=\"false\"; dispatch & poll & abort & result → 404. But note: `canceled` tombstone only set in onAbort which is switch-independent; with switch off, the result endpoint skips the tombstone branch → 404. Good.\n\nAlso with switch off, nextQueuedTask doesn't gate. Test bypass: dispatch with budgetMs=10 and immediately poll → hmm, can't deterministically observe. Just assert the switch function parses.\n\nTest 6 duplicate taskId: \n```ts\nconst ac1 = new AbortController();\nconst p1 = transport.dispatch({taskId:\"T-dup\", ..., budgetMs: 30}, ac1.signal);\np1.catch(()=>{});\nconst ac2 = new AbortController();\nconst p2 = transport.dispatch({taskId:\"T-dup\", ..., budgetMs: 5000}, ac2.signal);\nawait sleep(80); // p1 rejects (timeout), must not kill p2\nconst res = await poll(1);\nassert.equal(body.taskId, \"T-dup\");\nac2.abort(); await p2.catch(()=>{});\n```\nCareful: p1's timer fires at 30ms; the finish identity guard prevents deleting p2's entry. But the timeout test for p1 also splices ready if pending.get===entry — now guarded. Good. Note dispatch p2 calls flushPoll (no polls) → ready gets another \"T-dup\". After p1 timeout, ready still has one T-dup. Poll takes it and dedupes.\n\nBut wait: at 80ms p2's deadline is 5000 → active. Good.\n\nAlso test \"已认领不重复发\" via duplicate: after first poll, second poll → 204.\n\nLet me also test `inactiveReason` pure function quickly.\n\nNow write the test file."
      }
    ],
