      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Issue in test 2: after `new PairingStore(file)` (reloaded), calling `pairOne(file)` creates *another* PairingStore instance on the same file (\"重新配对\" store) which issues a code and redeems; `again` (third instance) is a separate in-memory copy that won't see the new device. I wrote `const fresh = pairOne(file, ...)` which uses its own store; then `again.verifyDeviceToken(fresh...)` — `again` was loaded before the new device was written → false. Fix: use `fresh.store.verifyDeviceToken(...)`.\n\nAlso \"重配仍可用\" — verifying with the same store that just redeemed.\n\nLet me fix.\n\nAlso in test 4: `one`/`pairOne` with switch off — fine.\n\nAnother issue: `pairOne` after `new PairingStore(file)` inside test 2: 3 instances of the same file all writing could clobber, but tests are sequential operations; the last save wins. Fine.\n\nFix the assertion."
      }
