      "reasoning": "Line 65: `assert.equal(store.verifyDeviceToken(redeemed.deviceToken), true);` after pairing a second device with a *different* PairingStore instance (`pairOne` creates a new store). So `store` (first instance) doesn't know the first device? No — the first device was created by `store` itself. Hmm, line 65 is after `const second = pairOne(file, \"第二台\")`. The second `pairOne` creates a new PairingStore from file, issues a code and redeems → saves file with 2 devices. But that doesn't affect `store` in memory, which still has 1 device with the original token. So verify should be true...\n\nWait, maybe line 65 is `assert.equal(store.verifyDeviceToken(second.deviceToken), true)` — the second device was created in the other instance's memory and saved to file, but `store`'s memory doesn't have it → false. Yes, line 65 is likely the second verify.\n\nFix: verify second token via the second store instance (`second.store`). Change pairOne return to include store (it does). Use `second.store.verifyDeviceToken`. And first: `store.verifyDeviceToken(redeemed.token)`.\n\nAlso `assert.notEqual(second.fingerprint, redeemed.fingerprint)` fine.",
