        "text": "So the error is thrown in the finally block at 2848, and it masks the original error that occurred after \"拿到会话的内外 id 映射\". The original error is swallowed? Node reports the finally's error, but the original error should still surface... Not necessarily; an error in finally replaces.\n\nSo what failed right after the id map? The next lines:\n```\nawait (await getHarness()).close(BACKGROUND_CONTEXT);\nconst { Store } = await import(\"../../../../../chancellor/src/store.ts\");\nconst { DelegationReceipts } = await import(\"../src/delegation-receipts.ts\");\n...\nconst store = new Store(TICKETS_ROOT);\n```\n`new Store(TICKETS_ROOT)` — TICKETS_ROOT = `.data/probe-http-tickets/runs`. It creates the dirs. Then saveTicket(fixture(\"T-source\"...)). \"Harness 失效\" is printed by close. Then something threw.\n\nMaybe the probe expects the dir to be removed; previously `rmSync(TICKETS_BASE)` at 1509 was before server restart. But the EPERM at 2848 suggests a handle is open on the directory. What opens `.data/probe-http-tickets` as a directory? The server process (killed?) No, the server from section 18 is still running with CHANCELLOR_WORK_ROOT=TICKETS_ROOT; chancellor's Store opens sqlite at `.data/probe-http-tickets/data/...` maybe. If the main body threw, finally kills server and sleeps 300ms, then deletion still fails because the server didn't die or file handles linger.\n\nKey question: what's the original error? It's masked. Let me run with a temporary modification? Better: check if this failure exists on the baseline (HEAD) — run `git stash`? We can't commit but can create a temp copy. Simplest: run the probe on HEAD version by using `git show HEAD:ts/apps/server/scripts/http-probe.ts`? But the failure might come from source changes (delegation-api/pairing), not the probe.\n\nLet me think: my `summarizeFanoutGroup` calls `new Store(workRoot)` and `new Store(runsDir)` on every request. In §18, the server is running with CHANCELLOR_WORK_ROOT=TICKETS_ROOT. The `/api/v1/tickets` list call also creates Stores. So nothing new.\n\nBut wait — `new Store` on a *non-existent* dir creates the sqlite file. My group route runs `summarizeFanoutGroup` only when the endpoint is called. Not yet called at the crash point.\n\nWhat about `pairing.ts` changes? The PairingStore constructor now calls `sealFingerprints()` → `persist()` → writes `.data/pairing.json`. Ah! The server at §18 uses default PAIRING_STORE_PATH (I set `PAIRING_STORE_PATH: \".data/probe-http-pairing.json\"` in startServer env). During startup, `pairing = new PairingStore()` runs `sealFingerprints`; with no devices, no write. Fine.\n\nHmm. But the probe process itself imports `../src/agent.ts` at 1540 — that import triggers `index.ts`?? No, agent.ts.\n\nActually, wait: the crash occurred while *importing* `../src/delegation-receipts.ts`? No.\n\nLet me get the original error by temporarily wrapping the finally rmSync in try/catch (which is arguably a good permanent fix for Windows flakiness, consistent with tests' approach). Then re-run to see the real error. I'll make the cleanup robust: a helper `clean(path)` with try/catch, since the probe already acknowledges Windows handle lag elsewhere (`maxRetries`).\n\nThat's a justified small change. Then rerun."
      }
