      "reasoning": "The dir contents exist. The EPERM is on deleting the dir itself. Could be a current process (the probe process's cwd?) — no. Maybe antivirus.\n\nThe critical question: does the baseline probe also fail? Let me check git status to see if probe file is modified (yes) and test the baseline with `git stash`.\n\nSafer approach: copy the current modified files aside, `git checkout -- ` the modified files (not destructive to my work if I keep copies), run probe, then restore. But untracked new test files don't matter for the probe. However source changes (delegation-api, pairing) also affect the running server. To test baseline entirely, I'd need to revert all source changes.\n\nAlternative: figure out the real main-body error. Let me make the finally cleanup resilient (try/catch per path) — this is a legitimate fix for Windows flakiness — then re-run to see the original error. If the original error is a genuine bug in my code, I'll see it.\n\nAdd a `cleanup()` helper:\n```ts\nfunction cleanup(path: string, options: Parameters<typeof rmSync>[1] = { force: true }): void {\n  try { rmSync(path, options); } catch { /* Windows 句柄回收慢半拍；探针断言已跑完，清理失败不判红 */ }\n}\n```\nand replace all rmSync in finally. That's a broad edit but mechanical. Maybe only wrap the whole finally body in try/catch around each line... Simplest: wrap the whole cleanup block in `try { ... } catch {}`. But then a failure mid-block leaves later files. Use a loop over paths.\n\nI'll restructure the finally: kill server, sleep, then loop over `[path, opts]` entries with per-entry try/catch. That's a moderate rewrite of ~30 lines. Acceptable and improves robustness.\n\nLet me view the whole finally block.",
