        "text": "Looks good. One concern: `markCanceled` loop logic — the for loop iterates and deletes expired ones while size > CAP; if all are fresh, deletes nothing, then the while loop drops oldest. Fine.\n\nAnother concern: `inactiveReason` checks `now >= deadlineAt`; dispatch timer uses `setTimeout(budgetMs)`. A task delivered at exactly deadline is dropped — correct.\n\nNow run test once more, then final answer. Also verify no stray processes from probe.\n\nAlso check that `pairing.ts` line endings changed? Warning: \"LF will be replaced by CRLF\" — my python write used newline='' preserving? The warning appears for files I edited. Not a problem (repo likely has autocrlf). But did my python rewrite change the file's line endings from CRLF to LF? I used `newline=''` which writes exactly what's in string (the read decoded with universal newlines → '\\n'). So the file may have converted CRLF → LF. Git warning says \"LF will be replaced by CRLF\" meaning working copy is LF now while index had CRLF? That would cause a huge diff? git diff --stat shows 128 changed lines for pairing.ts (reasonable, not whole file). So fine.\n\nRun final tests."
      }
